  Structured logs that only reach the user's own devtools console won't help you debug a production issue you didn't
  witness, so this choice decides whether the subsystem is "better console output" or "log delivery."

  - [1mConsole-only, structured[0m — a [38;5;153mlogger[39m module with levels, timestamps, and context fields; browser writes to [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m,
    Node writes JSON to stdout. Buys you consistency and log discipline cheaply, with zero dependencies and nothing to
    operate. Costs you: browser-side production issues remain invisible unless a user copies their console. Cheap to
    change later — adding a remote sink on top of a logger interface is additive.
  - [1mConsole + pluggable remote sink[0m — same logger interface, plus a transport seam. Browser batches warn/error (and
    optionally everything) to an HTTP endpoint; Node keeps stdout. Buys you actual production visibility. Costs: you
    need an endpoint to receive them, plus batching, failure handling, and a decision about PII. Hardest part to change
    later is the wire format.
  - [1mThird-party service[0m (Sentry, LogRocket, Datadog RUM) — buys you mature error capture, stack traces, and session
    replay for near-zero build effort. Costs a dependency, a vendor, and sending user data off-site. Rules out little,
    but it's the one that most constrains the design to the vendor's model.

  My recommendation: [1mconsole + pluggable remote sink[0m, built so the sink is optional and defaults to off. It's the only
  option that actually meets "debug production issues" for the browser, and designing the transport as a seam means you
  can point it at your own endpoint now and swap in Sentry later without touching call sites.

  One thing I'll flag regardless of your answer: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently logs the username on every attempt and receives a
  password in the same call. Any logging design here needs an explicit rule about redaction before we ship logs anywhere
  off the user's machine.
